WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.470
Instructor: All right, so now we're going to walk through

00:01.470 --> 00:03.780
some of the different ways of using git diff.

00:03.780 --> 00:06.690
We've got git diff, git diff HEAD,

00:06.690 --> 00:09.300
git diff staged, and cached.

00:09.300 --> 00:11.940
And then we have git diff HEAD with a file name,

00:11.940 --> 00:14.130
git diff staged with a file name,

00:14.130 --> 00:15.840
branch names that we can use.

00:15.840 --> 00:17.790
We can put commit hashes in there.

00:17.790 --> 00:19.710
So there's a lot that we can cover.

00:19.710 --> 00:21.840
It all follows a very similar pattern though,

00:21.840 --> 00:23.580
so it's not super difficult.

00:23.580 --> 00:26.130
But I'm going to begin by making a very basic repo

00:26.130 --> 00:29.280
that we can follow along with instead of this crazier one

00:29.280 --> 00:31.860
with all these changes and tons of files.

00:31.860 --> 00:34.050
I'm going to do just a trivial example.

00:34.050 --> 00:35.460
It all works the same way.

00:35.460 --> 00:37.560
So I'm not in a repo at the moment.

00:37.560 --> 00:39.000
I'll init a repo,

00:39.000 --> 00:44.000
and I'm going to make just a colors file, colors.txt.

00:44.250 --> 00:46.410
And why don't I add a commit right now?

00:46.410 --> 00:47.580
Git add

00:47.580 --> 00:49.980
colors.txt, git commit.

00:49.980 --> 00:51.633
We'll just do initial commit.

00:52.680 --> 00:55.350
I'm just going to make some basic history.

00:55.350 --> 00:56.820
And then here's my file.

00:56.820 --> 00:58.920
So I'll add red into that file,

00:58.920 --> 01:00.990
and then I'll do a commit -am,

01:00.990 --> 01:02.940
which just means add everything,

01:02.940 --> 01:06.213
and then M for message so I don't have to add in commit.

01:07.320 --> 01:09.180
I'm going to just do this very quickly.

01:09.180 --> 01:10.710
I'll probably fast forward.

01:10.710 --> 01:12.630
So this one will be add orange.

01:12.630 --> 01:15.420
I'm just going to build up the rainbow one commit at a time.

01:15.420 --> 01:17.420
Maybe I'll do yellow and green together.

01:18.360 --> 01:20.760
So this, let me clear,

01:20.760 --> 01:23.220
will be "add yellow

01:23.220 --> 01:24.387
and green",

01:25.410 --> 01:26.500
and then blue

01:27.810 --> 01:29.013
and purple.

01:31.080 --> 01:32.670
So I'll do the same thing.

01:32.670 --> 01:36.297
Add a commit message, "add blue and purple".

01:37.380 --> 01:39.390
Okay, so we've got our rainbow set up now

01:39.390 --> 01:41.253
and I have a tiny bit of history.

01:42.150 --> 01:44.490
I have what, five commits so far?

01:44.490 --> 01:47.250
Okay, that's enough for us to start using git diff.

01:47.250 --> 01:49.440
So the first version I showed you, the only version,

01:49.440 --> 01:51.840
is just git diff with nothing else,

01:51.840 --> 01:53.310
and this will show us all the changes

01:53.310 --> 01:55.890
in our working directory that are not staged

01:55.890 --> 01:57.270
for the next commit.

01:57.270 --> 02:00.720
So it compares staging and the working directory.

02:00.720 --> 02:02.370
Also, staging is usually referred

02:02.370 --> 02:05.400
to the index on the documentation, which is confusing.

02:05.400 --> 02:07.290
Staging makes more sense.

02:07.290 --> 02:08.760
So a lot of people say that.

02:08.760 --> 02:11.040
Right now, if I run it, there's no difference

02:11.040 --> 02:13.290
because there are no changes at all.

02:13.290 --> 02:14.670
There's nothing in the staging area,

02:14.670 --> 02:16.320
there's nothing in the working directory.

02:16.320 --> 02:17.733
Nothing has changed.

02:18.570 --> 02:21.573
So, if I instead do something simple like,

02:22.620 --> 02:24.240
well, what I did in the last video,

02:24.240 --> 02:26.250
if I remove purple,

02:26.250 --> 02:28.530
and replace it as

02:28.530 --> 02:29.363
indigo,

02:30.930 --> 02:32.223
let's just save that.

02:33.150 --> 02:35.250
If I type "git status",

02:35.250 --> 02:37.440
we see, all right, there's something that's been modified.

02:37.440 --> 02:39.780
And now if I type "git diff",

02:39.780 --> 02:42.000
we see an actual diff.

02:42.000 --> 02:45.030
So this is showing us a comparison between what the index

02:45.030 --> 02:47.612
knows about and what the staging area has

02:47.612 --> 02:50.160
versus the changes that we've made.

02:50.160 --> 02:52.680
Another way of thinking about it is that the differences

02:52.680 --> 02:56.370
are what we could tell git to add to the staging area.

02:56.370 --> 03:00.600
So as we see here in our colors.txt file,

03:00.600 --> 03:02.460
we're comparing one file to the next,

03:02.460 --> 03:04.920
first version is "A" second version is "B".

03:04.920 --> 03:08.670
So "A" is what the staging area knows about,

03:08.670 --> 03:12.990
versus "B" is the changes we have in our working directory.

03:12.990 --> 03:15.127
So "A" gets the minus sign, right?

03:15.127 --> 03:16.560
"B" gets the plus sign.

03:16.560 --> 03:19.140
So in the the version of our files

03:19.140 --> 03:21.423
that the staging area last knew about,

03:22.320 --> 03:24.540
purple was still there.

03:24.540 --> 03:26.280
That's what that minus sign means.

03:26.280 --> 03:27.990
Versus with the version

03:27.990 --> 03:30.600
of the file that is actually in our working directory,

03:30.600 --> 03:33.450
we see that indigo has been added.

03:33.450 --> 03:35.370
So that's what the plus indicate.

03:35.370 --> 03:36.900
It doesn't always mean something is new,

03:36.900 --> 03:39.330
it just means that it came from file "B",

03:39.330 --> 03:42.510
and in our case, file "B" are the new changes.

03:42.510 --> 03:44.160
So that's all I'll show you in this video.

03:44.160 --> 03:47.310
Just to make this point more time, git diff on it's own

03:47.310 --> 03:48.450
will list the changes in our

03:48.450 --> 03:51.153
working directory that are not staged.
